WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.805 --> 00:00:02.872
(ticking clocks)

00:00:02.872 --> 00:00:05.687
- You're not supposed to worry
about how the clocks got set,

00:00:05.687 --> 00:00:08.104
right, they just did get set.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.717
When everything's working
properly and I'm doing my job,

00:00:11.717 --> 00:00:14.467
you don't know anything about it.

00:00:19.748 --> 00:00:20.898
- [Interviewer] Are you wearing a watch?

00:00:20.898 --> 00:00:22.312
- Yeah, I don't wear a watch because it's

00:00:22.312 --> 00:00:23.618
too compulsive.

00:00:23.618 --> 00:00:25.307
- [Interviewer] That's Judah Levine.

00:00:25.307 --> 00:00:26.624
- It makes you look at the watch all

00:00:26.624 --> 00:00:29.033
the time and you kind
of go a little crazy.

00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:30.841
- [Interviewer] His nickname is literally

00:00:30.841 --> 00:00:32.683
the Nation's Time Lord.

00:00:32.683 --> 00:00:35.272
- I mean, that comes from
Dr. Who, if you've ever

00:00:35.272 --> 00:00:36.674
watched Dr. Who.

00:00:36.674 --> 00:00:38.260
I don't remember who
made me the Time Lord.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.997
I think it's unfair to all my colleagues.

00:00:41.997 --> 00:00:43.920
- [Interviewer] Do you have a Tardis?

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.301
- Uh no, I don't have a Tardis.

00:00:45.301 --> 00:00:47.617
Only Dr. Who has a Tardis.

00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:49.057
- [Interviewer] Okay
fair enough, but there's

00:00:49.057 --> 00:00:50.941
a reason he got that nickname.

00:00:50.941 --> 00:00:54.462
Judah's job is to officially
keep time standard.

00:00:54.462 --> 00:00:56.000
He works here at the National Institute

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.171
of Standards and Technology.

00:00:58.171 --> 00:00:59.975
It's a government agency
that maintains standards

00:00:59.975 --> 00:01:02.415
of all quantities, such as length, speed,

00:01:02.415 --> 00:01:03.969
and, well...

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:05.203
Time.

00:01:05.203 --> 00:01:09.456
- In the old days, which
means like 100 years ago,

00:01:09.456 --> 00:01:12.259
time was something that was astronomical.

00:01:12.259 --> 00:01:15.352
It was sunrise, or sunset
or noon or whatever.

00:01:15.352 --> 00:01:17.150
In the more modern era, time is something

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.011
defined by a ton of clocks, which provide

00:01:20.011 --> 00:01:22.979
a very stable reference frequency.

00:01:22.979 --> 00:01:24.590
- [Interviewer] Atomic
clocks are the most accurate

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.715
way to tell time on earth.

00:01:26.715 --> 00:01:29.595
45 years ago, Judah and
a team of scientists

00:01:29.595 --> 00:01:31.988
at NIST developed a system
for the atomic clock

00:01:31.988 --> 00:01:34.725
that tracks time more
accurately than ever before

00:01:34.725 --> 00:01:36.875
in the U.S., linking all phones,

00:01:36.875 --> 00:01:40.294
power grids, financial
markets, you name it.

00:01:40.294 --> 00:01:41.891
Every day he checks to
make sure the systems

00:01:41.891 --> 00:01:45.232
are calibrated, ensuring that
time is right on schedule.

00:01:45.232 --> 00:01:49.399
- These are the atomic clocks,
this is the real stuff.

00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:52.199
- [Interviewer] What if we switched off

00:01:52.199 --> 00:01:53.209
the atomic clock.

00:01:53.209 --> 00:01:54.571
What if it just stops working?

00:01:54.571 --> 00:01:57.029
- Uh, certainly cell phones systems stop

00:01:57.029 --> 00:01:58.194
pretty much right away.

00:01:58.194 --> 00:01:59.793
The internet stops pretty quickly.

00:01:59.793 --> 00:02:02.514
High end tech stuff would
suffer very quickly.

00:02:02.514 --> 00:02:04.135
- [Interviewer] But that's
probably not gonna happen.

00:02:04.135 --> 00:02:08.229
- There are 400 atomic clocks
in many timing laboratories.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:10.017
I don't think we're ever
going to intentionally

00:02:10.017 --> 00:02:12.317
switch off all of the atomic clocks.

00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:14.650
That's an unthinkable event.

00:02:15.914 --> 00:02:17.363
- [Interviewer] If you say so, Judah.

00:02:17.363 --> 00:02:18.783
After, all, you're the only reason we

00:02:18.783 --> 00:02:20.866
all know what time it is.

